WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.810
What if evolution could design a neural network for you?

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.513
Not just its weights, but its whole shape.

00:00:06.513 --> 00:00:09.271
This is NEAT, learning to land on the Moon.

00:00:09.271 --> 00:00:11.517
And this time, there's wind.

00:00:12.117 --> 00:00:13.893
This is Lunar Lander.

00:00:13.893 --> 00:00:17.753
The goal is to touch down softly between the two flags.

00:00:17.753 --> 00:00:22.892
Gravity pulls the lander down, and every burst of an engine costs fuel.

00:00:23.492 --> 00:00:26.898
At every step, the lander senses eight numbers.

00:00:26.898 --> 00:00:34.319
Where it is, how fast it is moving, how it is tilted and spinning, and whether each leg touches the ground.

00:00:34.919 --> 00:00:38.599
And at every step, it chooses one of four actions.

00:00:38.599 --> 00:00:44.545
Do nothing, fire the left engine, fire the main engine, or fire the right engine.

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:46.822
And now there is wind.

00:00:46.822 --> 00:00:52.469
Gusts push the lander sideways, and turbulence twists it, and both keep changing as it flies.

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:55.097
The arrow in the corner shows the wind.

00:00:55.097 --> 00:00:57.557
But the lander can't see that arrow.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:00.017
Wind is not one of its eight inputs.

00:01:00.017 --> 00:01:03.783
It can only notice that it is drifting, and push back.

00:01:04.383 --> 00:01:07.038
The game hands out points as it goes.

00:01:07.038 --> 00:01:11.403
Moving toward the pad, slowing down and staying level earn points.

00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:15.184
Drifting away, speeding up and tilting cost points.

00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:17.993
There are also one-off events.

00:01:17.993 --> 00:01:20.957
Each leg that touches the ground earns ten.

00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:23.224
Coming to rest earns a hundred.

00:01:23.224 --> 00:01:25.384
A crash costs a hundred.

00:01:25.984 --> 00:01:27.757
And fuel isn't free.

00:01:27.757 --> 00:01:31.665
Every frame the main engine fires costs a little.

00:01:32.265 --> 00:01:34.430
We add one thing of our own.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:40.987
A landing earns a bonus of up to fifty, the full amount exactly in the middle, and nothing at a flag.

00:01:40.987 --> 00:01:46.279
So evolution is pushed to land in the centre, not just anywhere on the pad.

00:01:46.879 --> 00:01:52.430
An average game score of two hundred, without our bonus, counts as solved.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:55.586
The pilot is a small neural network.

00:01:55.586 --> 00:01:58.200
The eight inputs come in on the left.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.484
They flow along weighted connections, and come out on the right as four scores, one per action.

00:02:05.084 --> 00:02:07.918
The action with the highest score fires.

00:02:07.918 --> 00:02:11.288
Watch the scores change as the lander flies.

00:02:11.888 --> 00:02:15.984
This network has three hidden nodes and twenty-two connections.

00:02:15.984 --> 00:02:23.598
Two of those nodes are memory nodes that every network was given at the start, and that evolution was not allowed to delete.

00:02:23.598 --> 00:02:25.502
Nobody designed the rest.

00:02:25.502 --> 00:02:30.186
Evolution found every weight, and decided which connections to keep.

00:02:30.786 --> 00:02:33.889
Evolution doesn't work on the network directly.

00:02:33.889 --> 00:02:36.485
It works on a genome: a list of genes.

00:02:36.485 --> 00:02:38.911
Node genes say which neurons exist.

00:02:38.911 --> 00:02:43.651
Connection genes say which neurons are wired together, and with what weight.

00:02:43.651 --> 00:02:46.942
Read the list, and you can build the network.

00:02:47.542 --> 00:02:52.787
Each connection gene also carries a historical marker, called its innovation number.

00:02:52.787 --> 00:02:57.919
It is given out the first time that connection appears anywhere in the population.

00:02:57.919 --> 00:03:02.309
So when two genomes share a number, those genes have the same origin.

00:03:02.309 --> 00:03:08.139
The library we use names each gene by the two nodes it connects, which does the same job.

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:13.172
Each new genome starts as a copy of a parent, and then it mutates.

00:03:13.172 --> 00:03:15.988
There are three kinds of mutation.

00:03:16.588 --> 00:03:19.752
The most common one simply nudges a weight.

00:03:19.752 --> 00:03:23.340
Here, a positive connection becomes negative.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.402
The second kind adds a connection, joining two nodes that were not connected before.

00:03:29.402 --> 00:03:32.077
It gets a new innovation number.

00:03:32.677 --> 00:03:34.604
The third kind adds a node.

00:03:34.604 --> 00:03:37.246
It splits an existing connection in two.

00:03:37.246 --> 00:03:41.155
The old gene is switched off, and two new genes take its place.

00:03:41.155 --> 00:03:49.413
The connection into the new node gets a weight of one, and the one out keeps the old weight, so the network behaves almost the same as before.

00:03:49.413 --> 00:03:52.961
The structure has grown, without breaking anything.

00:03:53.561 --> 00:03:55.885
Two parents can also be combined.

00:03:55.885 --> 00:03:58.890
This is where the innovation numbers pay off.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:04.519
Line the genes up by their numbers, and you can see which genes the two parents share.

00:04:05.119 --> 00:04:08.228
Genes that both parents have are called matching.

00:04:08.228 --> 00:04:12.374
A gene missing in the middle of the other parent is called disjoint.

00:04:12.374 --> 00:04:16.547
And a gene beyond the end of the other parent is called excess.

00:04:17.147 --> 00:04:21.298
The child takes each matching gene from either parent at random.

00:04:21.298 --> 00:04:25.103
The disjoint and excess genes come from the fitter parent.

00:04:25.103 --> 00:04:30.822
No matter how different the two networks look, the child is always a sensible network.

00:04:31.422 --> 00:04:32.892
Here is a problem.

00:04:32.892 --> 00:04:39.677
A mutation that adds a node usually makes the network worse at first, because its new weights are not tuned yet.

00:04:39.677 --> 00:04:45.123
Ranked against everyone, it would be thrown out before it could ever become useful.

00:04:45.723 --> 00:04:48.932
So NEAT measures how different two genomes are.

00:04:48.932 --> 00:04:56.751
It counts the genes that don't match, adds the weight differences of the genes that do, and divides by the size of the genome.

00:04:56.751 --> 00:05:02.010
If that distance is small enough, the two genomes belong to the same species.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:06.309
Genomes then compete mostly inside their own species.

00:05:06.309 --> 00:05:12.978
Each species shares its offspring among its members, so a big species can't crowd out a small new one.

00:05:12.978 --> 00:05:17.826
And a species that stops improving for twenty generations dies out.

00:05:18.426 --> 00:05:21.366
So here is one generation of NEAT.

00:05:21.366 --> 00:05:27.686
First, evaluate: every one of the 150 genomes flies, and gets a fitness score.

00:05:28.286 --> 00:05:32.557
Then, speciate: sort the genomes into species.

00:05:33.157 --> 00:05:41.241
Then, select: inside each species, the best ones become parents, and the two very best are kept unchanged.

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:46.574
And reproduce: crossover and mutation fill the population back up.

00:05:46.574 --> 00:05:48.985
Then it all starts again.

00:05:49.585 --> 00:05:55.632
Fitness is the average over five flights of the game score plus the centre bonus.

00:05:56.232 --> 00:06:03.845
Every genome in a generation flies the same starting positions, in the same wind, so the comparison is fair.

00:06:03.845 --> 00:06:09.854
The starts and the winds change each generation, so no genome can memorise one.

00:06:10.454 --> 00:06:15.199
But five windy flights are noisy, and the top genome is often just lucky.

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:20.823
So the ten best genomes of each generation fly thirty more flights before they are kept.

00:06:20.823 --> 00:06:26.798
And each generation, the champion also flies a hundred flights that no genome ever trained on.

00:06:26.798 --> 00:06:32.253
We only call the problem solved when that unseen game score reaches two hundred.

00:06:32.853 --> 00:06:36.168
Now the real run, fifteen hundred generations.

00:06:36.168 --> 00:06:40.159
Every dot is one genome, and its color shows its species.

00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:47.833
In generation zero, every network is the inputs wired to the outputs, plus the two memory nodes, with random weights.

00:06:47.833 --> 00:06:50.159
Almost all of them crash.

00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:54.946
Generation by generation, the cloud of dots creeps upward.

00:06:54.946 --> 00:07:02.161
By generation one hundred and twenty-four, the champion scores a hundred and fifty on unseen flights.

00:07:02.761 --> 00:07:05.348
Progress is slow, but it keeps coming.

00:07:05.348 --> 00:07:10.186
Only after a thousand generations does the champion pass a hundred and ninety.

00:07:10.186 --> 00:07:18.566
The best, from generation one thousand and ninety, scores a hundred and ninety-nine on its hundred unseen flights, just short of two hundred.

00:07:18.566 --> 00:07:20.535
After that, it slides back.

00:07:20.535 --> 00:07:23.985
In wind, this problem is still not quite solved.

00:07:24.585 --> 00:07:26.973
This is the classic NEAT picture.

00:07:26.973 --> 00:07:31.399
Each band is one species, and its height is how many genomes it has.

00:07:31.399 --> 00:07:34.028
At the start, there is just one.

00:07:34.628 --> 00:07:40.421
New species split off, grow when they do well, and shrink and die when they stop improving.

00:07:40.421 --> 00:07:45.168
Over the whole run, one hundred and seventeen species came and went.

00:07:45.768 --> 00:07:48.830
Here is one generation flying together.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:54.367
All 150 genomes start from the same spot, and we draw each flight as a trail.

00:07:54.367 --> 00:07:56.752
This is generation zero.

00:07:57.352 --> 00:08:01.518
At first, nearly every flight ends in a crash.

00:08:03.952 --> 00:08:08.358
A few dozen generations in, many trails curve down toward the pad.

00:08:08.358 --> 00:08:13.839
The wind bends them, and they spread out much more than they would in calm air.

00:08:14.439 --> 00:08:21.710
And late in the run, a share of the population lands from this start, but the trails never bundle into one tidy path.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.967
Every gust asks for a slightly different answer.

00:08:24.967 --> 00:08:29.629
Mutation keeps breaking things, and that is the price of searching.

00:08:30.229 --> 00:08:33.397
This is how the champion's network changed.

00:08:33.397 --> 00:08:40.220
It started fully connected, with all eight inputs wired to all four outputs, and no hidden nodes.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:44.623
Adding nodes was made rarer, so the networks stayed small.

00:08:44.623 --> 00:08:53.380
The winner, from generation one thousand and ninety, has three hidden nodes, two of them the protected memory nodes, and twenty-two connections.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:57.656
Whether it actually uses that memory, we'll test in a moment.

00:08:58.256 --> 00:09:04.172
Here are the champions from different generations, all flying the same windy start.

00:09:04.772 --> 00:09:07.506
The early ones crash or drift away.

00:09:07.506 --> 00:09:12.830
Later champions fight the drift, slow down, and land between the flags.

00:09:26.626 --> 00:09:28.705
So what did evolution build?

00:09:28.705 --> 00:09:33.509
Let's switch connections off one at a time, and see which ones matter.

00:09:34.109 --> 00:09:40.469
The most important connection runs from the falling speed to the main engine, with a big negative weight.

00:09:40.469 --> 00:09:45.252
When the lander falls fast, the main engine's score shoots up, and it brakes.

00:09:45.252 --> 00:09:49.717
Switch this one connection off, and the lander never lands at all.

00:09:50.317 --> 00:09:53.738
Next, the right leg sensor votes for doing nothing.

00:09:53.738 --> 00:09:57.448
Once a leg is down, the lander stops firing and settles.

00:09:57.448 --> 00:10:01.458
Without it, only eighteen percent of these flights land.

00:10:02.058 --> 00:10:04.889
And the tilt angle feeds the right engine.

00:10:04.889 --> 00:10:07.947
This is part of what keeps the lander upright.

00:10:07.947 --> 00:10:11.135
Without it, only twenty-eight percent land.

00:10:11.735 --> 00:10:18.331
Here are the connections that matter most, ranked by how much the landing rate drops without them.

00:10:18.931 --> 00:10:23.101
Twelve of the twenty-two connections can go with almost no loss.

00:10:23.101 --> 00:10:26.180
A handful of them carry the whole skill.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.338
And the hidden nodes, memory included?

00:10:29.338 --> 00:10:34.119
Cut all three out, and on a hundred test flights the lander does just as well.

00:10:34.119 --> 00:10:38.233
Eighty-five percent landings without them, eighty-three with them.

00:10:38.233 --> 00:10:43.537
Evolution kept the memory nodes because it had to, but the skill doesn't use them.

00:10:44.137 --> 00:10:46.267
So, is the wind hard enough?

00:10:46.267 --> 00:10:54.259
Here is the champion from the calm-air version of this project, flying in wind, next to the new winner, on the same start.

00:10:55.256 --> 00:11:02.081
In calm air, the old champion landed ninety-five percent of the time, with a score of two hundred and fifty-four.

00:11:02.081 --> 00:11:06.312
In wind, it drops to sixty-four percent, and a hundred and sixteen.

00:11:06.312 --> 00:11:14.678
The new winner, trained in wind, lands eighty-three percent, with a score of a hundred and eighty-two, and more landings in the middle.

00:11:15.278 --> 00:11:17.459
So why is it still not solved?

00:11:17.459 --> 00:11:23.828
Our first wind run had stalled too, so we recorded a thousand of its flights and studied how they fail.

00:11:23.828 --> 00:11:26.124
Gusts that come and go are fine.

00:11:26.124 --> 00:11:32.793
But when the wind keeps pushing from one side for most of a flight, the lander fails every single time.

00:11:33.393 --> 00:11:36.075
And evolution itself was being fooled.

00:11:36.075 --> 00:11:38.641
Five windy flights are a lucky draw.

00:11:38.641 --> 00:11:45.055
The genome NEAT picked as best, in orange, always did much worse on a hundred fresh flights, in green.

00:11:45.055 --> 00:11:50.011
Often a better genome, in blue, was right there in the population, unnoticed.

00:11:50.011 --> 00:11:56.550
That is why the later runs give the top genomes thirty extra flights, and add new nodes less often.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.588
The steady push has a deeper cause.

00:11:59.588 --> 00:12:06.732
Hovering still in calm air, and hovering still against a steady wind, give the network exactly the same eight numbers.

00:12:06.732 --> 00:12:10.530
But one needs no engine, and the other needs the engine on.

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:15.422
A network that only reacts to the moment has to answer both the same way.

00:12:16.022 --> 00:12:18.214
Engineers fix this with memory.

00:12:18.214 --> 00:12:24.621
A loop lets a node keep a running total of the drift, and keep pushing back for as long as the wind lasts.

00:12:24.621 --> 00:12:27.543
It is the integral part of a PID controller.

00:12:27.543 --> 00:12:29.173
So we tried it twice.

00:12:29.173 --> 00:12:31.533
One run was allowed to grow loops.

00:12:31.533 --> 00:12:38.970
The final run started every network with two memory nodes, each looping to itself, and evolution could not delete them.

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:43.575
Here are all four wind runs, flying the same thousand flights.

00:12:43.575 --> 00:12:46.265
The first run lands sixty-nine percent.

00:12:46.265 --> 00:12:48.782
A steadier search gives seventy-one.

00:12:48.782 --> 00:12:50.842
Allowing loops gives eighty.

00:12:50.842 --> 00:13:01.212
And the final run, with protected memory and fifteen hundred generations, lands eighty-five percent, with an average score of a hundred and ninety-one, the best by far.

00:13:01.812 --> 00:13:03.929
But memory was not the reason.

00:13:03.929 --> 00:13:07.049
In the loop run, evolution threw the loops away.

00:13:07.049 --> 00:13:11.951
In the final run, switching the memory off changes nothing, not a single flight.

00:13:11.951 --> 00:13:15.906
The gain came from the steadier search and the longer training.

00:13:15.906 --> 00:13:19.771
And a strong, steady wind still beats every one of them.

00:13:20.371 --> 00:13:30.105
On a hundred new windy flights, the winner lands eighty-three percent of the time, thirty-two percent in the middle, with an average score of a hundred and eighty-two.

00:13:30.105 --> 00:13:31.996
Close, but not yet solved.

00:13:31.996 --> 00:13:37.024
And it got there with a network that, in practice, only reacts to the moment.

00:13:37.624 --> 00:13:41.682
The code, the training run, and these videos are all on GitHub.

00:13:41.682 --> 00:13:45.526
Clone it, train your own, and watch your network grow.
